    {
      "id": "be-s12m-monopoly-c2",
      "heading": "Marginal revenue and profit maximization",
      "explanation_md": "A monopoly can charge more than MC, but **not anything it likes\\**: the higher the price, the less it sells.\n\nTo sell one more unit it must **lower the price on every unit\\**, so **MR is below the demand curve\\**. For a straight-line demand **P = a - bQ\\**, MR has the same start but **twice the slope\\**: **MR = a - 2bQ\\**.\n\nMR and elasticity are linked: where demand is **elastic\\** MR is positive and revenue rises; at **unit elastic\\** MR = 0 and **total revenue is highest\\**; where **inelastic\\**, MR is negative.\n\n**The two-step rule:**\n\n1. Find the output where **MR = MC\\**.\n2. Go **up to the demand curve\\** to read the price.\n\nProfit = **(P - ATC) x Q**.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "MR = MC for quantity, demand curve for price. Linear demand P = a - bQ gives MR = a - 2bQ.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
