      "worked": {
        "question_md": "Given P = 10 - Q and C(Q) = 6 + 2Q. Find the optimal output, price and profit.",
        "steps": [
          {
            "title": "MR",
            "body_md": "P = 10 - Q, so with a = 10 and b = 1: **MR = 10 - 2Q\\**.",
            "why": "MR = a - 2bQ for straight-line demand.",
            "result": "MR = 10 - 2Q"
          },
          {
            "title": "MC",
            "body_md": "C(Q) = 6 + 2Q, so **MC = 2**.",
            "why": "Each extra unit adds 2 to cost.",
            "result": "MC = 2"
          },
          {
            "title": "MR = MC",
            "body_md": "10 - 2Q = 2, so 2Q = 8 and **Q = 4**.",
            "why": "Profit is highest where the last unit adds as much revenue as cost.",
            "result": "Q = 4"
          },
          {
            "title": "Price from demand",
            "body_md": "P = 10 - 4 = **$6**.",
            "why": "Charge what buyers will pay for 4 units.",
            "result": "P = $6"
          },
          {
            "title": "Profit",
            "body_md": "| Item | Working | Value |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 6 x 4 | 24 |\n| Cost | 6 + 2 x 4 | 14 |\n| Profit | 24 - 14 | **10** |",
            "why": "Revenue minus cost.",
            "result": "Profit = $10"
          }
        ],
        "answer_md": "Q = 4 units, P = $6, profit = **$10**."
      },
      "watch_out": [
        "Put Q back into the **demand** equation for price: P = 10 - 4, not MR.",
        "Include the fixed cost of 6 in total cost."
      ],
      "memory_trick": "",
      "exam_answer_md": "MR = 10 - 2Q; MC = 2. Set MR = MC: 10 - 2Q = 2, so **Q = 4**. P = 10 - 4 = **$6**. Profit = PQ - C(Q) = (6)(4) - (6 + 8) = **$10**."
    },
    {
      "id": "be-s12m-monopoly-c4",
      "heading": "Long run, deadweight loss and the case for monopoly",
      "explanation_md": "In the long run a monopoly **keeps its profit\\**: nothing changes **unless the source of its power is removed\\**.\n\nGovernments dislike this because **P > MC\\**: some buyers would happily pay more than it costs to make another unit, but the monopolist doesn't sell to them. Output is **too low\\** and the price **too high\\**. The value of those missing trades is the **deadweight loss of monopoly\\**.\n\nIn the slide's example, a competitive market would produce where demand meets MC (Q = 8), but the monopolist stops at 4.\n\n**The other side:** economies of scale, scope and cost complementarities can make a monopoly cheap enough to outweigh the harm, and monopoly profits **encourage innovation\\**.\n\n**Two plants:** a monopoly with two factories sets **MR = MC1 = MC2**, and charges the price for the total Q = Q1 + Q2.",
      "example_ids": [
        "be-ex-electricity-two-plants"
      ],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "Monopoly: P > MC, too little output, deadweight loss. Two plants: MR = MC1 = MC2.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
